    "note": "Allocations that are the same in all three ITU Regions, for use when the user's region is unknown. This is the DEFAULT plan, chosen because it is the only one that is never wrong: the regions genuinely disagree about 40 m, 80 m, 160 m, 6 m, mediumwave and the FM broadcast edges, so any regional plan shown to an unknown user is incorrect somewhere. Every band edge was read from the Table of Frequency Allocations in Article 5 of the ITU Radio Regulations, Edition of 2024 (in force 1 January 2025, incorporating WRC-23), and an entry was included only where the Region 1, Region 2 and Region 3 columns share a single un-split row; cross-checked against the FCC Online Table of Frequency Allocations (47 CFR 2.106, revised 1 July 2022), which reproduces the same International Table. Maritime entries follow ITU Radio Regulations Appendices 15 and 18; aeronautical entries follow Appendix 27 and ICAO Annex 10 Volumes I and V, with the 1090 MHz emission bandwidth from Report ITU-R M.2205; ISM bands are those designated worldwide by RR 5.150; GNSS signal edges are from IS-GPS-200N, IS-GPS-705J, the GLONASS ICD L1/L2 Edition 5.1 and the Galileo OS SIS ICD Issue 2.2; time-station carriers are from NIST, NPL and PTB; ISS frequencies are from ARISS. Where a single designated frequency is shown rather than an allocated band (time-station carriers, distress and calling channels, ISS downlinks), the displayed width is a READABILITY CONVENTION centred on the correct frequency, not a regulatory figure; the exceptions are 121.5 MHz, whose 121.450-121.550 MHz span is the ICAO unassignable guard band, and the 25 kHz maritime VHF channels. INFORMATIONAL ONLY - a display aid, not an authoritative or licensing reference. JSON has no comment syntax, so this note lives in the data; the loader ignores unknown keys.",
